cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use these tools or notes, please cite them as below."
title: "how-sure: small tools and notes from a petroleum geologist's work"
abstract: "Small tools and notes from my work as a petroleum geologist. Each one deals with the same question: how sure are we, and what would make us surer? Tool 01 is a post-drill look-back template in Excel (prediction vs. result) with a hand-worked example and 32 checks. Tool 02 is an interactive page that checks a planned magnetic survey against one geological question (a dyke, a sill edge or a plug: visible or not, one anomaly or two, line spacing), with 24 checks against known answers. Tool 03 is an interactive page on planning a shallow stratigraphic drilling campaign when only a few wells are allowed (what each next well buys, how sure each core is to land in the unit it is meant for and date the horizons around it within the errors of the seismic pick, dip, cover and thickness and the chance that each unit gives an age, and how the first cores change the wells still to drill), with 49 checks against hand calculations and an independent count, and a one-page summary. Tool 04 is an interactive page and a Monte Carlo spreadsheet for a prospect's oil in place as a range, P90 to P10 (why the low case is not every input at its low end, which inputs drive the range, and which data could change the decision), with 74 checks on the page against hand calculations and an independent calculation, and a one-page summary. Tool 05 is an interactive page that checks one fault before it goes into a fault framework (which intervals it grew through, which of those calls are larger than the pick uncertainty, and whether the reservoir faces its seal across the fault), with 58 checks against hand calculations and an independent calculation, and a one-page summary. Tool 06 is an interactive page that compares the values of one well across two or three documents (well file, drilling report, summary table) before historical well data go into a database: it names the cause of each difference (another coordinate system, X and Y swapped, a zone number in front of the easting, depths from ground level, from another rig's KB or in feet, a magnetic survey or one written 180° off, tops written as elevations) and leaves typing errors, versions of a pick, core below TD and a possible second bore to the geologist. It takes one well in a form or many from a table and gives the accepted values as CSV files and a GeoPackage layer with their source and pending decisions, with 130 checks against hand calculations, the planted errors and an independent calculation, and a one-page summary. Tool 07, Is the answer on the page it cites?, is an acceptance check for any system that searches a document library and answers questions with a page reference. The user writes about forty questions of seven kinds (a fact from a page, a number from a table, units and depth reference, two documents that disagree, no answer in the documents, a figure or map, a comparison of two documents), asks each once and gives each answer one of six grades. The result is one line: answers from the right page, refusals where the documents have no answer, and made-up answers. Acceptance requires the whole exact binomial (Clopper-Pearson) range of the right-page share to clear a bar, no made-up answer and at least ten questions without an answer. Includes an offline HTML page, an Excel workbook and an independent Python check; the example run is made up. Brief 09, Geological brief for a UAV magnetic survey, is a template that a geologist fills before any survey design and gives to a licensed survey operator: the geological question and the decision, the target and its expected response, the survey requirements, the limits for accepted data, the stop points and who is responsible for what. A filled example uses the made-up dyke of tool 02; section 5 says what each possible result means before the survey is flown. Limits taken from Geoscience Australia Record 2023/04 and Geological Survey of Canada Open File 7660 are named; the rest are marked as assumed. PDF and Word, English and Russian; every computed number of the example is recomputed by check_numbers.py (ALL 44 PASS). The brief belongs to a proposed programme: no surveys have been flown. Tool 11 is an interactive page for a sill whose top is known from seismic: it compares the gravity and magnetic signal with the noise of ground, UAV and fixed-wing surveys, shows what one field alone cannot tell apart, and uses a linearised least-squares analysis to show which pair of field and data (seismic top, a well) pins the sill thickness. It carries 38 checks against hand answers and an independent calculation, and a one-page summary. Tool 12 is an interactive page for a high mapped under a dolerite sill: it computes the velocity pull-up the sill can make in time and in depth, compares it with the height of the high, shows the same sill in clastic, salt and dolomite host rocks, and shows how the uncertainty of the sill velocity changes the answer. It carries 26 checks against hand answers and an independent calculation, and a one-page summary."
